  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nA weak trough shifting east across the Great Lakes is supporting\nscattered thunderstorms across the region early this morning. Rich\nmoisture is also supplying locally heavy downpours. Expectations into\nsunrise are that the overall coverage will decrease as the trough \nshifts eastward. A weak boundary, most noticeable by viewing theta-e\nvalues in the low-levels, gradually moves into the area from the\nnorthwest this afternoon and evening. Temperatures will warm up and\nmodest surface dewpoints will still be present, allowing for isolated\nto scattered thunderstorm development by late afternoon and into the\nevening. The ultimate coverage of thunderstorms may be a bit limited\nby the lack of upper level forcing associated with the trough.\nHowever, the convergence along the boundary may help to focus more\neffective low level forcing with a destabilized air mass. Given these\nfactors, coverage is anticipated to be less than it was during the\nday on Friday. \n\nShifting into Sunday morning, the boundary remains stalled over the \narea. Southwesterly winds increase during the afternoon, pushing the\nboundary northward. The southwesterly flow also brings a renewed \ninflux of moisture across the Midwest. Aloft, a longwave trough over \nthe Hudson Bay in Canada will supply a steady stream of persistent \nnorthwesterly flow that lasts through midweek. Disturbances within \nthis northwesterly flow will initiate multiple episodes of organized \nconvection over the broader region. As is often the case with this \nscenario, day to day changes in the forecast across the local area \nshould be expected as confidence increases or decreases depending on \nthe thunderstorm evolution the previous day. Future SPC outlooks will\nalso shift around as a result. For now, Monday through Wednesday are\nthe days to watch, with the potential decreasing later in the week \nas the trough sinks farther south, bringing in drier and cooler air. \nThe exact impacts to the local area will vary from day to day, but \none or multiple damaging wind events are certainly possible. Given \nthis potential for multiple rounds, flash flooding related to \nexcessive rainfall is also a potential impact. The HWO remains broad \nwith the overall wording, given the uncertainty with this pattern.",
